 "decisions": [
  {
   "title": "One transaction for the whole transfer",
   "context": "The legacy opens a SqlTransaction right after the connection (line 34), validates inside it, and commits only after debit, credit and registration (line 102). Four rejections roll back (lines 43, 52, 62, 70); the same-account check (RULE-005) happens before the transaction begins.",
   "decision": "TransferFunds runs in a single transactional boundary generated from unit_of_work. Business errors roll everything back and are mapped to stable error codes. There are no savepoints, so no UnitOfWork port is needed. SAME_ACCOUNT is checked before any data access, as in the legacy.",
   "consequences": "Debit, credit and registration are atomic. Rejections leave the tables unchanged, as the traces show."
  },
  {
   "title": "Data migrates to the target database",
   "context": "The legacy accesses SQL Server tables CUENTAS and TRANSFERENCIAS directly and calls no stored procedures. Its only other logic is ComisionService, which is in-process code.",
   "decision": "data_strategy is migration: both tables move to the target database as cuentas and transferencias, with the legacy precisions (DECIMAL(13,2), DECIMAL(11,2), DECIMAL(7,2), VARCHAR(10), CHAR(1), CHAR(3), INT). Keys are unchanged.",
   "consequences": "A one-off data migration is required. Replay compares the tables before and after against the recorded traces."
  },
  {
   "title": "Commission as a pure domain calculation",
   "context": "ComisionService.Calcular is a static in-process function (uplift), not an external program.",
   "decision": "It is implemented as the CalculateCommission use case, exposed also as GET /commissions. TransferFunds reuses the same calculation inside its transaction. It uses BigDecimal only, with HALF_UP rounding to scale 2 (equivalent to MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero). The order is: base fee, cap at 5.00, then the INM surcharge of 1.00 with an exact, case-sensitive comparison.",
   "consequences": "There is no floating point and no rounding drift. An INM transfer can pay up to 6.00. Commission is never credited to any account (RULE-009)."
  },
  {
   "title": "Relative balance updates and sequential numbering kept as in the legacy",
   "context": "The legacy updates balances with CTA_SALDO = CTA_SALDO ± valor and numbers transfers with ISNULL(MAX(TRF_NUMERO),0)+1, both inside the transaction.",
   "decision": "The same semantics are kept: relative UPDATEs and MAX+1 read inside the transaction, starting at 1. No sequence or generator is introduced.",
   "consequences": "Behaviour matches the traces. The MAX+1 scheme carries the legacy's concurrency weakness: two concurrent transfers may compute the same number, and the primary key rejects the second one. Changing this would be a separate decision."
  },
  {
   "title": "Page flow becomes a REST contract; presentation validators are not translated",
   "context": "After commit the legacy sets lblComision and lblMensaje and immediately redirects to Comprobante.aspx?numero=N, so the user never sees those labels. The pages also carry UI validators (10-digit origin account, required fields, amount range 1 to 10,000) that are not part of the approved rules.",
   "decision": "POST /transfers returns numero, comision and mensaje 'TRANSFERENCIA REALIZADA', and the client navigates to GET /transfers/{numero}/receipt (the receipt). The page validators stay in the UI layer and are not added to the domain, so no behaviour beyond the approved rules is invented. Unparseable numeric input is rejected by the framework as a malformed request (400).",
   "consequences": "The observed outputs (lblMensaje, lblComision, numero) can be replayed from the API response. Domain rules only enforce what the legacy handler enforces."
  }
